 "title": "Why Firing Your Worst Client Makes You More Money",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "Some customers cost more time and effort than they are worth, meaning they actually lose you money. By calculating your true hourly pay per client, you can identify your worst clients and either fix their terms or fire them to make room for better ones.",
 "key_points": [
  "Some customers cost more than they pay when factoring in extra time spent texting, chasing payments, or doing free work.",
  "Calculate what each client pays you per hour to find your least profitable ones.",
  "The creator highlights 'The Whale Curve': a few customers make most of your profit, while a few at the bottom actually lose it.",
  "You can fix a bad client by setting new rules, such as requiring payment on the day and charging extra for extra rooms.",
  "If a bad client refuses your new terms, let them go so you can free up time for a new client who pays on time.",
  "Start by evaluating your worst customer and decide whether to fire them or fix them."
 ],
 "content": "Two cleaners, same [music] suburb, same rates. One fired her worst client and made more money. Both have the exact same [music] client. Texts at 10:00 at night, pays a month late, always wants one more room for free. Cleaner one keeps her. Money is money. Cleaner two does the math. 4 hours of cleaning, two more hours of texts and chasing. That job pays less than her cheapest [music] client. So, she sends a new price with clear rules. Pay on the day. Extra rooms [music] cost extra. The client says no. Fine. Now Thursday is free. A new client takes it. Pays on time. Accountants call this the whale curve. A few customers make most of your profit and a few [music] at the bottom actually lose it. Some customers cost more than they pay. Work out what each one pays you per hour and start at the bottom. So, your worst customer, fire them or fix them.",
